{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And they said one to another, 'It is man.'</b> Some say that the letters מָ״ן are the letters of the nose, for when a person closes his nose he cannot pronounce מֵ״ם and נוּ״ן with clear articulation; and the manna had within it the fragrance of all foods in the world, but in their eyes they saw nothing but this manna, as it is written (Numbers 11:6), ״בִּלְתִּי אֶל הַמָּן עֵינֵינוּ.״ And they called it מָ״ן to say that just as these two letters — their pronunciation is not discernible when the nose is closed, so too the manna, which contained only the fragrance of all foods — its perfection was nullified by the closing of the nose." "However, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 75a) that it also possessed the sense of taste of every kind of food, so that the palate would taste in it every kind of flavor. Therefore they said one to another 'מָן הוּא,' an expression meaning 'preparation of food' — that is to say, it contained the preparation of every kind of food in the world, so that whoever tasted it felt as though he were eating of everything. And they had not yet given it its unique name, for they did not know what it was. Nor did they yet call it by the name 'manna'; they only said among themselves, 'It is מָן,' but they did not yet give it this name until after the day of Shabbat, as it is written, ״וַיִּשְׁבְּתוּ הָעָם בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי״ (Exodus 16:30), and afterwards it is written, ״וַיִּקְרְאוּ שְׁמוֹ מָן״ (Exodus 16:31). For on the sixth day a double and twofold portion descended; therefore they called it by the name מָ״ן, for these are among the doubled letters, when you write מֵ״ם נוּ״ן — on account of this miracle, that it came down doubled on the sixth." "And as for what is written, <b>״וַיִּקְרְאוּ בֵית יִשְׂרָאֵל שְׁמוֹ מָן,״</b> and it does not say 'the children of Israel' — this is because 'the house of Israel' means the women, as Rashi explained on the verse ״כֹּה תֹאמַר לְבֵית יַעֲקֹב״ — 'these are the women' (Exodus 19:3). And the meaning is that the women cherished the manna most of all; therefore they gave it a name indicating the endurance of the matter, for they wanted it to endure because it made known the righteousness of the women, as is found in tractate Yoma (75a): ״וְהַמָּן כִּזְרַע גַּד״ — it would make known who had sinned against whom, the husband against his wife or the wife against him. If the manna was found in her father's house, then surely her husband had sinned against her, and likewise the reverse, etc. Therefore the women cherished it in order to make known their righteousness, for in most cases the women are bested by the men. And this is what is written: ״אִישׁ לַאֲשֶׁר בְּאָהֳלוֹ תִּקָּחוּ״ — for the sake of his wife who is in his tent: for if it appears to him that his wife is rebelling against him and there are no witnesses to the matter, then he may take the manna, for it makes known everything that occurs in his tent. And from this the expression ״לַאֲשֶׁר בְּאָהֳלוֹ״ is precisely inferred, and immediately adjacent to it is ״וְהַמָּן כִּזְרַע גַּד,״ for this depends on that. And on this basis it appears to me that one may explain the word ״מָן״ as in what is written (Exodus 10:8), ״מִי וָמִי הַהֹלְכִים,״ whose Targum is ״מַן וּמַן״ — so too here, it would make known who had sinned against whom, and that is the meaning of ״מָן.״ And according to what the Gemara concludes there, that it would make known whether a child was formed in the ninth month from the first husband or in the seventh from the second, it appears to me that ״מָן״ is from the root מִנְיָן, for by means of it they were able to count whether it was the ninth or the seventh month."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The second citation (Numbers 11:6) was given without a verse address in the source; the address is supplied from the parallel in comment [1] and the known biblical reference.", 1 "The source gives the Talmud reference without a leaf number; the standard page reference is Yoma 75a, supplied here as in comment [2].", 2 "The source cites Rashi on Exodus 19:3 but the verse address is implicit in the phrase ״כֹּה תֹאמַר לְבֵית יַעֲקֹב״; supplied from the known biblical text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:01.421870492Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}